nanimous Tossup, with one Tilt R", "Polls split along sponsor lines; most recent independent poll gives Ford a narrow edge", "Late seven-figure outside spending boosting Ford"], "rationale": "Every signal points to a coin-flip: fundamentals give Lombardo a modest incumbency edge, the polling average is within a point of even, six of seven raters call it a Tossup, and prediction markets price the race between 48 and 55 percent for Ford. Fresh Democratic outside money and a strong September Emerson poll offset the incumbent's structural advantages, leaving the race essentially dead even with a slight Republican tilt.", "watch": "A late-breaking poll from a high-quality nonpartisan pollster, or the outcome of the proposed Lombardo-Ford debate, could break the deadlock in either direction.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Joe Lombardo (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Nevada's governor's race between Republican incumbent Joe Lombardo and Democratic Attorney General Aaron Ford is one of the tightest contests of 2026. The state's near-even partisan lean, Lombardo's incumbency advantage, and Ford's late surge backed by more than $1 million in outside Democratic spending have kept both sides within the margin of error all cycle.\n\nPolling has been sparse and sponsor-divided: Republican-affiliated surveys showed Lombardo up by 7 to 12 points in the summer, while the most recent independent Emerson poll put Ford ahead by two. Averages sit within half a point of even, forecasters overwhelmingly rate the race a Tossup, and prediction markets price it between 48 and 55 percent for Ford. The forecast lands on a razor-thin Republican edge with wide uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.4762, "margin": -0.32}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.447, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4885, "margin": -0.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.4837, "margin": -0.4}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.4293, "margin": -0.7}, {"date": "20